text:
toni: Heads-up: a customer reported our SDK bundles `undici` into a Vercel Edge deployment and it blows past size limits. We need a short, copy-paste section for the SDK README that explains: (1) how we avoid shipping undici to edge runtimes, (2) how to keep TS types stable across Node/edge, and (3) what to do if you're stuck on Node14/16.

adrian: Short answer: do not import undici at top-level. Provide a factory that prefers an injected fetch, then globalThis.fetch, then a lazily imported undici. Also mark undici as an optionalDependency or peerDependency so package managers don't always install it for edge builds. Example consumer-friendly factory: 
```js
// createFetch.js - consumer + SDK use
export function createFetch(override) {
  return async function fetchWrapper(input, init) {
    if (override) return override(input, init);
    if (globalThis.fetch) return globalThis.fetch(input, init);
    // dynamic import keeps bundlers from including undici in edge builds
    const { fetch } = await import('undici');
    return fetch(input, init);
  }
}
```
This pattern also makes testing easier because users can pass a fake fetch.

lee: Packaging tips: conditional exports are your friend, but also include a `browser` field or `exports` map entry that points to a small shim for edges (which should not import undici). Example `package.json` snippet: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs",
    "default": "./dist/index.node.cjs"
  },
  "./fetch-shim": {
    "import": "./dist/fetch-shim.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/fetch-shim.cjs"
  }
},
"browser": {
  "./dist/fetch-shim.cjs": "./dist/fetch-shim.mjs"
}
```
Where `fetch-shim.mjs` is a tiny module that just re-exports `globalThis.fetch`, so bundlers targeting browsers/edges pick the ESM shim.

toni: Good. What about TypeScript consumers that don't have DOM libs? We keep getting `RequestInit`/`Response` missing errors.

adrian: Two pragmatic options: ship minimal ambient types and document the preferred tsconfig. For minimal types, include `types/web-fetch.d.ts` in the package and point `types` in package.json or use `exports` to expose them. Minimal example: 
```ts
// types/web-fetch.d.ts
interface _RequestInit { headers?: Record<string, string> | Array<[string, string]>; body?: any; }
export type FetchLike = (input: string | URL, init?: _RequestInit) => Promise<any>;
export default FetchLike;
```
This is intentionally tiny; it avoids forcing consumers to enable \"lib\": [\"DOM\"]. Also add a note: \"If you want full DOM types, add `lib: ['DOM']` to your tsconfig or install `@types/web`.\"

lee: CI/testing matrix: add Node 14, 16, 18 to the matrix and a small smoke test that runs in a Cloudflare Worker emulator (or use wrangler/dkt) to ensure our edge build doesn't contain `undici`. We spy on bundle contents (`npm pack` + tar inspection) in the pipeline: fail if `undici` appears in edge artifact.

devrel: UX suggestion for README snippet: provide three copy-paste recipes: 
1) Edge deployers (Vercel/Cloudflare/Deno/Bun): use ESM bundle, rely on global fetch, and do NOT install undici. Minimal example to put in README: 
```js
import { createFetch } from 'redwood-sdk';
const fetch = await createFetch(); // will use global fetch in edge
```
2) Node 18+ users: no action needed, SDK will use global fetch.
3) Node 14/16/older: `npm install undici` (or have undici optionalDependency) and SDK will lazy-load it. Or inject a fetch: `const f = require('node-fetch'); const fetch = createFetch(f);`

toni: Any gotchas with bundlers like webpack/rollup that could still inline undici?

adrian: Yes: static analysis can inline imports if you import the module symbolically somewhere else in your bundle. Avoid `import 'undici'` anywhere in top-level shared code. Also, some packagers follow `optionalDependencies` and still include. Use dynamic import strings (`await import('undici')`) and ensure the SDK entrypoints that bundlers resolve for edges point to the shim file that doesn't reference undici at all.

lee: Another tip: mark undici as `optionalDependencies` and add a runtime check that when the dynamic import fails (module not installed) we surface a clear runtime error telling the user to install undici or inject a fetch. Example: 
```js
try { const { fetch } = await import('undici'); return fetch; }
catch (err) { throw new Error('No fetch available. Install `undici` or pass a fetch to createFetch().'); }
```

ci-cd-bot: Pipeline note: added job "bundle-scan" to run on PRs that checks for `undici` in edge-targeted artifacts.

devrel: I'll draft the README section and a short troubleshooting FAQ: "Edge bundle too large? Check for undici imports, check package-lock/optionalDependencies, and ensure you use the ESM edge entrypoint." I'll also add the tiny ambient types and a TS example for how to pass an injected fetch to keep types clean.

toni: This is perfect 44d thanks. I'll paste this into the SDK repo and open a follow-up issue to add the optionalDependency + CI job.

